"The word 'zalzalah' mentioned in the first ayah of Surah az-Zilzal refers to the violent shaking of the earth. This shaking is the great earthquake that will occur on the Day of Resurrection. The terror of this earthquake is also described in Surah al-Hajj, Surah al-Waqi'ah, and other surahs.\n\nIn the second ayah, the earth bringing forth its burdens has been interpreted in two ways: first, the resurrection of the dead from their graves; second, the emergence of the treasures and minerals within the earth. Scholars who combine both views have stated that the word 'athqal' encompasses both the dead and the treasures. Whether this bringing forth occurs at the first blowing of the Trumpet or the second is disputed.\n\nIn the third ayah, it is described that man, in the face of this terrifying event, is bewildered and asks, 'What is the matter with this earth?' This expression emphasizes the horror of the earthquake and the bringing forth. In this situation, the disbelievers are gripped by fear, while the believers realize that Allah's promise has been fulfilled.\n\nIn the fourth and fifth ayahs, it is stated that on that Day the earth will recount all its news, because its Lord has inspired it. This recounting may be metaphorical, or it has also been interpreted as Allah literally causing the earth to speak. As narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him), the earth will bear witness to the deeds that everyone committed upon it.\n\nIn the sixth ayah, it is stated that people will emerge in scattered groups, in various states: some with honor and some in disgrace; some mounted and some on foot; some felicitous and some wretched. Every community will be summoned with their leaders, or every individual will arrive alone.\n\nIn the seventh and eighth ayahs, it is explained that deeds will be displayed and that an atom's weight of good or evil will be recompensed. The purpose is to demonstrate that even the smallest good or evil is not lost in the sight of Allah. The atom (zarrah) is an exceedingly small entity and represents the lowest limit of accountability."